# Copy to .env for local development:  cp .env.example .env
#
# These are read by the server-side functions only (netlify/functions/*.mjs).
# None are prefixed with VITE_, so Vite will never include them in the browser
# bundle. Do not add a VITE_ prefix to any of them — that would publish them.
#
# On Netlify, set the same variables under:
#   Site configuration -> Environment variables

# --- Anthropic, for the career match, resume and interview features ---
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# --- AutoTrain, for the strand classifier ---
#
# REQUIRED: your AutoTrain login. The prediction endpoint wants the same
# bearer token the AutoTrain dashboard sends — a Firebase ID token, good for
# one hour — so the function signs in for itself through Firebase's documented
# REST endpoint and renews from there. Nothing has to be copied out of a
# browser, and nothing goes stale.
AUTOTRAIN_EMAIL=
AUTOTRAIN_PASSWORD=

# OPTIONAL. The AutoTrain project's public web API key (starts AIza). Firebase
# documents this as an identifier rather than a secret — every visitor to the
# dashboard already has it. Left unset, the function reads it from the
# AutoTrain app itself; set it to skip that lookup, or if the app changes shape
# and the lookup stops finding it. Either spelling is accepted.
# AUTOTRAIN_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
# AUTOTRAIN_FIREBASE_APIKEY=

# OPTIONAL. The prediction endpoint, which names the training job:
#   https://api.autotrain.app/api/autotrain/jobs/<job id>/predict?access=dashboard
# Compiled into classify.mjs. Retraining issues a new job id and the old URL
# then 404s — set this to the fresh one when that happens.
# AUTOTRAIN_URL=

# OPTIONAL, and no longer needed. A Firebase refresh token, still honoured so
# an older deployment keeps working. Signing in does not require it.
# AUTOTRAIN_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# AUTOTRAIN_API_KEY and AUTOTRAIN_AUTH are gone. They held a pasted ID token,
# which expired an hour after it was copied. Delete them from Netlify.

# Treat AUTOTRAIN_PASSWORD as a secret: it is your AutoTrain login. .env is
# git-ignored; on Netlify these live in the site's environment.
